In article <32dfgg$3ld@pheidippides.axion.bt.co.uk>, nwinton@axion.bt.co.uk (Neil Winton) writes:
> Here's a question for those brave adventurers who have explored deepest
> mysteries of DCE security :-)
> 
> Is it possible to add a "foreign" principal (ie a principal from a remote
> cell) into a local group? Assuming that it is, does group-based access
> control work correctly in these circumstances?
> 
It is not possible to do this. The structure of a PAC, however, still
has room for foreign groups. It will always be empty, since the PAC is
constructed by the privilege service, and it will not have foreign
group members.

The foreign_* ACL entries work just fine, As long as your client
(acl_edit) can bind to the cell containing the foreign_* that you are
placing on the ACL.

> The documentation of routines such as sec_rgy_pgo_is_member() doesn't give
> a warm feeling as to this being possible but I'd like to find out whether
> this has been tried. If it doesn't work I'd also like to know if anyone can
> explain why such a design decision was taken (other than "it was too difficult
> to get it working before the code was shipped" :-)
> 
One thing that strikes me as somewhat difficult is maintaining
referrential integrity across cells. If principal p in cell A belongs
to foreign_group /.../cell-B/group - how does cell A's registry get
notified when the group in cell B is deleted? Within a cell this
integrity is constantly maintained by the registry.

