In article <JCONNO03.94Aug17131300@pluto.cps.plnin.gmeds.com> jconno03@cps.plnin.gmeds.com (John Connolly) writes:
>I am trying to avoid the limitations of the DCE thread stack.  For
>pipes, we cannot transmit the data as a linked list, which is one of
>the reasons to avoid the use of pipes!

You don't need to use pipes. Just use [transmit_as()] IDL
attribute. For instance,

    const long MAX_LIST_LEN = 100000;

    typedef struct xmit_t
    {
        long last;
        [last_is(last)] long values[MAX_LIST_LEN];
    } xmit_t;

    typedef [transmit_as(xmit_t)] struct list_t
    {
        long value;
        struct list_t *next;
    } list_t;

    long send_it
    (
        [in] handle_t h,
        [in] struct list_t list
    );

Then, you provide these functions:
    void list_t_from_xmit(xmit_t *xmit_object, list_t *object);
    void list_t_to_xmit(list_t *object, xmit_t **xmit_object);
    void list_t_free_inst(list_t *object);
    void list_t_free_xmit(xmit_t *xmit_object);

(See the IDL generated header.)

---  Seiichi

P.S.
  The un/marshaling of the linked list is done by recursively
  calling the pointer un/marshaling routine. Thus, you hit the
  stack limit (deep call stack).
