In article <tlihsin.776363209@hubcap>, tlihsin@hubcap.clemson.edu (lih-sin the) writes:

|>  I would like to know how to format and mount a 3.5 inch floppy disk on
|>an OSF/1 system. Can we read and write a DOS formatted floppy disk on
|>OSF/1 system?

To format a floppy, use /sbin/scu, the SCSI control utility.  Since the
floppy drives are SCSI devices, a floppy will appear as a /dev/rrz*
device.  If the floppy is /dev/rrz3c, then you can do the following

	/sbin/scu -f /dev/rrz3c format density ED

to format a floppy at extra density.  WARNING: this program will format
*any* SCSI device and destroy all existing data on that device in the
process.  Issue this command with EXTREME care.

For more information, check the manual page for scu(8).

While you can't mount a floppy directly with normal DEC OSF/1, you can
use the freeware mtools software 
(gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/GNU/mtools-2.0.7.tar.gz) to read and write them.

If you need more direct access, a product like SoftPC or SoftWindows
from Insignia will effectively emulate an entire PC environment.  There
you'll be able to access the floppy exactly as you would on a PC.

					Frank



